h1. Spring Boot Application Logs Integration with GCP Cloud Logging via Custom Logging Service

h2. Overview

This guide demonstrates how to forward Spring Boot application logs to Google Cloud Logging using a custom logging service deployed on GCE VMs and google-fluentd agent. The solution leverages Splunk client library for efficient log forwarding and ensures proper timestamp handling in GCP Cloud Logging.

h2. Architecture Overview

{panel:title=System Components}
* Spring Boot Applications with Splunk client library
* Custom Logging Service deployed in Managed Instance Group (MIG)
* Built-in google-fluentd Agent on GCE VMs
* Google Cloud Load Balancer
* Google Cloud Logging
{panel}

{panel:title=Data Flow}
1. Applications generate logs and send them to Custom Logging Service via Splunk client library
2. Custom Logging Service writes logs to local files using RollingFileAppender
3. google-fluentd monitors and reads log files using tail plugin
4. google-fluentd processes and forwards logs to GCP Cloud Logging
5. Logs become available in GCP Log Explorer with original timestamps
{panel}

h2. Prerequisites

* Google Cloud Platform Account with:
** Compute Engine MIG with google-fluentd installed
** Cloud Logging API enabled
** Cloud Load Balancing configured
* Java Development Kit (JDK) 17
* Maven >= 3.8

h2. Implementation Guide

h3. 1. Custom Logging Service Configuration

h4. 1.1 Log File Configuration

The Custom Logging Service uses RollingFileAppender to write logs to local files:

{code:xml}
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<configuration>
    <appender name="FILE" class="ch.qos.logback.core.rolling.RollingFileAppender">
        <file>/var/log/custom-logging-service/application.log</file>
        <rollingPolicy class="ch.qos.logback.core.rolling.TimeBasedRollingPolicy">
            <fileNamePattern>/var/log/custom-logging-service/application.%d{yyyy-MM-dd}.log</fileNamePattern>
            <maxHistory>7</maxHistory>
        </rollingPolicy>
        <encoder class="net.logstash.logback.encoder.LogstashEncoder">
            <includeMdcData>true</includeMdcData>
            <timestampPattern>yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss.SSSX</timestampPattern>
        </encoder>
    </appender>

    <root level="INFO">
        <appender-ref ref="FILE"/>
    </root>
</configuration>
{code}

h4. 1.2 google-fluentd Configuration

Create a configuration file for tailing application logs:

{code:properties}
# /etc/google-fluentd/config.d/custom-service-tail.conf
<source>
  @type tail
  format json
  path /var/log/custom-logging-service/application.log
  pos_file /var/lib/google-fluentd/custom-service.pos
  tag custom.service
  read_from_head true
</source>

<match custom.service>
  @type google_cloud
  
  use_metadata_service true
  detect_json true
  enable_monitoring false
  
  # Timestamp handling
  adjust_invalid_timestamps false
  timestamp_key timestamp
  
  # Resource and labels configuration
  resource_type "gce_instance"
  insert_id_key log_id
  labels_key log_labels
  
  <buffer>
    @type file
    path /var/lib/google-fluentd/custom-service-buffers
    flush_interval 5s
    chunk_limit_size 2M
    total_limit_size 512M
    retry_max_interval 30
    retry_forever false
  </buffer>
</match>
{code}

h3. 2. Spring Boot Application Configuration

h4. 2.1 Maven Dependencies

{code:xml}
<dependencies>
    <!-- Splunk Client Library -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>com.splunk.logging</groupId>
        <artifactId>splunk-library-javalogging</artifactId>
        <version>1.8.0</version>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
{code}

h4. 2.2 Application Configuration (application.yml)

{code:yaml}
spring:
  application:
    name: your-app-name

logging:
  splunk:
    host: ${LOGGING_SERVICE_HOST}
    port: ${LOGGING_SERVICE_PORT}
    token: ${LOGGING_SERVICE_TOKEN}
{code}

h3. 3. Infrastructure Configuration

h3. 3.1 Managed Instance Group Setup

* Configure instance template with:
** Custom Logging Service deployment
** google-fluentd installation and configuration
** Appropriate IAM roles
** Health check endpoints

h3. 3.2 Load Balancer Configuration

* Setup HTTP(S) Load Balancer
* Configure backend service with MIG
* Setup health checks
* Configure round-robin load balancing

h3. 3.3 Autoscaling Configuration

* Define CPU utilization target
* Set minimum and maximum instances
* Configure cooldown period

h2. Deployment Steps

h3. 1. Prepare Deployment Artifacts

The following artifacts need to be prepared:
* Custom Logging Service JAR file
* Service configuration files
* SSL certificates
* google-fluentd tail configuration
* Terraform scripts for infrastructure provisioning

h3. 2. Automated Deployment Process

The deployment process is automated using Ansible and Terraform, following these steps:

h4. 2.1 Ansible Preparation Phase
* Upload deployment artifacts to GCP bucket:
** Custom Logging Service JAR
** Configuration files
** SSL certificates
** google-fluentd tail configuration
* Clone Terraform repository containing infrastructure code

h4. 2.2 Terraform Infrastructure Provisioning
The Terraform scripts handle the following tasks in sequence:

1. Initial VM Creation and Configuration
* Create temporary VM instance
* Configure VM with startup scripts for Custom Logging Service
* Install and configure google-fluentd
* Setup necessary IAM roles and permissions

2. Custom Image Creation
* Create custom image from configured VM
* Validate image creation
* Clean up temporary VM

3. Instance Template Creation
* Create instance template using custom image
* Configure metadata and startup scripts
* Set up networking and security configurations

4. MIG and Load Balancer Setup
* Create Managed Instance Group
* Configure auto-scaling policies
* Setup HTTP(S) Load Balancer
* Configure health checks
* Setup backend services

h3. 3. Verify Deployment

{code:bash}
# Verify MIG deployment
gcloud compute instance-groups managed list-instances <mig-name>

# Check load balancer health
gcloud compute backend-services get-health <backend-service-name>

# Verify auto-scaling configuration
gcloud compute instance-groups managed describe <mig-name>

# Test logging service endpoints
curl -k https://<lb-address>/health
{code}

h3. 4. Post-Deployment Configuration

Update application configuration with load balancer details:

{code:yaml}
logging:
  splunk:
    host: ${LOGGING_SERVICE_LB_ADDRESS}
    port: 443  # Default HTTPS port
    token: ${LOGGING_SERVICE_TOKEN}
{code}

h2. Verification

h3. 1. Check System Components

{code:bash}
# Check MIG status
gcloud compute instance-groups managed list-instances <mig-name>

# Check load balancer health
gcloud compute backend-services get-health <backend-service-name>

# View google-fluentd logs
sudo tail -f /var/log/google-fluentd/google-fluentd.log
{code}

h3. 2. View Logs in GCP Console

1. Navigate to Cloud Logging Console
2. Use the following query to filter logs:
{code:text}
resource.type="gce_instance"
jsonPayload.app_name="your-app-name"
{code}

h2. Best Practices

* Monitor MIG metrics for scaling decisions
* Implement proper error handling in Custom Logging Service
* Use structured logging with consistent field names
* Include correlation IDs for request tracing
* Set appropriate log retention policies
* Monitor google-fluentd buffer usage
* Implement proper security measures for logging service access

h2. Troubleshooting

h3. Common Issues

|| Issue || Solution ||
| Logs not appearing in GCP | Check google-fluentd service status and tail configuration |
| Load balancer health check failures | Verify Custom Logging Service health endpoints |
| High latency | Check MIG scaling settings and current load |
| Connection refused | Verify load balancer and firewall configurations |

h3. Useful Commands

{code:bash}
# Check MIG and instances
gcloud compute instance-groups managed describe <mig-name>

# View load balancer logs
gcloud compute url-maps list

# Check google-fluentd configuration
sudo google-fluentd --dry-run -c /etc/google-fluentd/google-fluentd.conf
{code}

